WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.950
Multi-GPU success depends on the software stack understanding the available devices.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:09.120
Drivers, compute runtimes, inference engines, model formats, operating system,

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.000
and libraries must agree on supported hardware and features.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.425
CUDA is a common NVIDIA compute platform.

00:00:14.425 --> 00:00:18.240
ROCm supports selected AMD hardware and operating-system combinations.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.995
Vulkan can provide cross-platform acceleration in compatible runtimes.

00:00:21.995 --> 00:00:24.750
Capability and maturity vary by device and workload.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.870
Apple Metal supports acceleration on Apple platforms,

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:31.905
usually through integrated unified-memory designs rather than a conventional collection of

00:00:31.905 --> 00:00:32.630
discrete cards.

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:37.040
Multi-device expectations from desktop GPU systems should not be assumed to transfer

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:37.641
directly.

00:00:37.641 --> 00:00:42.790
llama.cpp provides multi-GPU concepts such as device selection and model splitting for

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:43.750
supported backends.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:48.420
Server-oriented runtimes such as vLLM may offer parallel execution and scheduling at a

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:51.570
high level, with different hardware and configuration requirements.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:56.225
Configuration includes visible devices, memory limits, split strategy, cache, precision,

00:00:56.225 --> 00:00:58.640
batch behavior, networking, and service controls.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.240
A copied command may be wrong for a different topology or runtime version.

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:06.340
Maintain a known working combination of drivers, runtime, model, configuration,

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:10.090
and operating system. Test upgrades, record versions, preserve rollback,

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:12.170
and avoid changing several layers at once.

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:15.200
Software stability can determine whether expensive hardware is useful.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:19.220
Containers can improve repeatability but do not erase host-driver, device, kernel,

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:20.481
and runtime requirements.

00:01:20.481 --> 00:01:24.590
Record both host and container environments, device visibility, mounted models,

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:26.470
permissions, and launch configuration.
